Translation patch: <RistrettoPoint as VartimeMultiscalarMul>::optional_multiscalar_mul (src/ristretto.rs)

Change: The lazy `points.into_iter().map(|opt_P| opt_P.map(|P| P.0))` becomes a
`Vec<Option<EdwardsPoint>>` built by a nested helper.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided
methods (`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`/`skip` on adapters, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the
`IntoIter` of a generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id ..
from ExtractBase.Item"), so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function
generic over `It: Iterator`, called with `x.into_iter()`.

Behaviour: same result: the callee receives the same `Option`s in the same order and
returns `None` iff one of them is `None`. The points are read eagerly (all of them, even
after a `None`) before the callee reads the scalars; this is only observable through an
input iterator with side effects.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

diff --git a/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs b/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
index 269662d..29f52a1 100644
--- a/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
+++ b/curve25519-dalek/src/ristretto.rs
@@ -984,23 +984,34 @@ impl VartimeMultiscalarMul for RistrettoPoint {
     fn optional_multiscalar_mul<I, J>(scalars: I, points: J) -> Option<RistrettoPoint>
     where
         I: IntoIterator,
         I::Item: Borrow<Scalar>,
         J: IntoIterator<Item = Option<RistrettoPoint>>,
     {
-        let extended_points = points.into_iter().map(|opt_P| opt_P.map(|P| P.0));
+        fn extended_points<It>(mut points: It) -> Vec<Option<EdwardsPoint>>
+        where
+            It: Iterator<Item = Option<RistrettoPoint>>,
+        {
+            let mut out = Vec::new();
+            while let Some(opt_P) = points.next() {
+                out.push(opt_P.map(|P| P.0));
+            }
+            out
+        }
+
+        let extended_points = extended_points(points.into_iter());
 
         EdwardsPoint::optional_multiscalar_mul(scalars, extended_points).map(RistrettoPoint)
     }
 }
 
 /// Precomputation for variable-time multiscalar multiplication with `RistrettoPoint`s.
 ///
 /// Note that for large numbers of `RistrettoPoint`s, this functionality may be less
 /// efficient than the corresponding `VartimeMultiscalarMul` implementation.
 // This wraps the inner implementation in a facade type so that we can
 // decouple stability of the inner type from the stability of the
 // outer type.
 #[cfg(feature = "alloc")]
 pub struct VartimeRistrettoPrecomputation(crate::backend::VartimePrecomputedStraus);
 
